// Audio gain-apply — the M11 demo that shows TWO structurally distinct
// cross-worker transport shapes in ONE Nucleus application.
//
// Structurally this is a near-clone of example 02-split-add (top-level
// loads, a per-element loop, a top-level save). The arithmetic is a
// Q8 fixed-point gain: `out[i] = (samples[i] * gains[i]) >> 8`, with
// `gains[i] = 256` meaning unity gain. The point of the example is NOT
// the arithmetic — it is what the *schedule* does with the data edges.
//
// What this example demonstrates
// ------------------------------
//   - DMA-async vs PIO-sync transport, side by side, from ONE source.
//     In `schedules/dma_pio.sched.nuc` the bulk audio frames cross as
//     `mode=dma` edges (samples in, out back) while the per-sample gain
//     coefficients cross as a `mode=pio` edge. The embedded-pattern
//     backend (TASK-0438.02) emits a STRUCTURALLY DIFFERENT code path
//     for each: a DMA edge renders `shim.dma_link_arm(...)` +
//     `dma_link_recv_arm(...)` plus a completion spin loop; a PIO edge
//     renders the unchanged `shim.link_push` / `shim.link_recv` byte
//     loop. Both ride the SAME UART fabric and are value-equivalent, so
//     the whole app stays Renode byte-exact (the proof of the demo).
//   - The bulk=DMA / control=PIO split mirrors a real embedded audio
//     pipeline (ex14 hearing-aid reality): you DMA a frame buffer but
//     PIO a handful of control words.
//
// HONEST caveat — Renode DMA is a TIMING MODEL, not a silicon engine
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Under the Renode co-sim the modelled DMA does NOT run a real STM32H7
// DMA stream. `dma_link_arm` delegates to the same `link_push` UART
// transport, and `dma_link_poll` returns `true` synchronously, so the
// emitted spin loop terminates on its first iteration. That means the
// proof this example carries is VALUE-correctness (byte-exact output),
// NOT timing-correctness (parent TASK-0438 AC#4). A real async DMA
// engine — where `dma_link_poll` reads the silicon transfer-complete
// bit and the CPU does useful work while the stream runs — is the
// explicit follow-up TASK-0048.12. See `README.md` and TASK-0438.03.
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate
// --------------------------------------
//   - Real cycle-accurate DMA timing (see the caveat above; TASK-0048.12).
//   - Distributed placement across many compute workers (a single `w0`
//     runs the whole loop, exactly like 02-split-add).
//   - Buffered / notify=event async transfers. `mode=` is orthogonal to
//     `sync`/`async`; here both edges stay `sync` so the synchronous
//     embedded shim can honour them honestly.
//
// Numeric choice: i32 (PRD §10.1 — integers are bit-deterministic by
// language definition). The Q8 multiply uses `wrapping_mul` on the Rust
// side so overflow is two's-complement deterministic, not UB.
//
// Dataflow shape (deliberately parallel to example 02-split-add)
// --------------------------------------------------------------
//   samples <-- load_samples();            // produced on host
//   gains   <-- load_gains();              // produced on host
//   for i : 0 .. N {
//       out[i] <-- apply_gain(samples[i], gains[i]);  // on w0
//   }
//   save_output(out);                      // consumed on host
//
// The algorithm has NO knowledge of `host`/`w0` or of DMA vs PIO. The
// placement AND the transport mode live entirely in the schedule
// (PRD §2 algorithm/schedule split; PRD §6.3.4 transport is a codegen
// concern, not an algorithmic one).

const N : usize = 256;     // 256 * 4 bytes per array = 1 KiB audio frame.

// Whole-program input/output arrays. Single-assignment per PRD §6.2.1.
data samples : i32[N];     // bulk audio frame (DMA-async edge in)
data gains   : i32[N];     // per-sample gain coefficients, Q8 (PIO-sync edge)
data out     : i32[N];     // processed audio frame (DMA-async edge back)


// Scalar Q8 gain-apply. Pure: reorderable, deduplicable, eliminable.
// Signature is explicitly scalar so the contract pass matches it
// against `pub fn apply_gain(sample: i32, gain: i32) -> i32`.
kernel apply_gain : (i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// I/O kernels. Effectful: ordering preserved, never duplicated.
// Aggregate-typed in Nuc; Rust side is `Vec<i32>` (see kernels.rs).
kernel load_samples : ()       -> i32[N] effectful;
kernel load_gains   : ()       -> i32[N] effectful;
kernel save_output  : (i32[N]) -> ()     effectful;


// Dataflow.
samples <-- load_samples();
gains   <-- load_gains();

for i : 0 .. N {
    out[i] <-- apply_gain(samples[i], gains[i]);
}

save_output(out);
